96-`SSHTransportDependencies` MUST contain candidate-neutral seams for:
97-
98-- TCP connection/readiness, so EAGAIN/event-loop readiness and cancellation are
99-  testable without sleeps;
100-- `SSHHostKeyPolicy`, invoked with raw evidence before credentials are requested;
101-- `SSHCredentialProvider`, invoked only after host acceptance;
102-- monotonic `TunnelClock`, cancellation checking, logger, typed observer/metrics,
103:  and secure identity generation;
104-- an optional experiment recorder whose candidate label is harness metadata and
105-  never visible to ordinary `ReluxTunnelCore` consumers.
106-
107-All public values are `Sendable`. Engine handles, event loops, channels, error
108-objects, key objects, and allocator pointers remain adapter-private.
109-
110-## 4. Configuration, identities, and capability report
111-
112-`SSHConnectionConfiguration` supplies the canonical SSH endpoint, username,
113-opaque credential reference, allowed KEX/host-key/cipher/MAC algorithms,
114:`SSHTimeoutPolicy`, `SSHRekeyPolicy`, and `SSHKeepalivePolicy`. All numeric policy
115-values are caller supplied and validated before any network work. No production
116-default is selected here.
117-
118-`SSHLaneIdentity` is an immutable opaque identifier generated by the runtime or
119-harness. It MUST NOT encode hostname, username, profile name, destination,
120-fingerprint, or credential reference. It is the only lane dimension permitted
121-in product metrics. `SSHChannelIdentity` is random, connection-local, and used
122-only to correlate lifecycle events; it has no routing meaning.
123-
124-Each factory exposes a harness-only `SSHAdapterCapabilities` value using common
125-enums. It reports algorithm and feature support without leaking candidate
126-types. Unsupported required behavior produces the stable
127-`unsupportedCapability`/`algorithmUnavailable` error and a red matrix row; it is
128-not a candidate-specific skip. In particular, RSA-SHA2 support by libssh2 and
129-its absence in the audited NIOSSH baseline are represented as data while
130-Ed25519 plus an approved product fallback remains the selection gate.
131-
132-## 5. Connection lifecycle and state ownership
133-
134-The observable connection states are:
135-
136-```text
137-idle -> resolving -> tcpConnecting -> keyExchange
138-     -> awaitingHostDecision -> authenticating -> ready
139-ready <-> rekeying
140-any nonterminal state -> closing -> closed
141-any nonterminal state -> failed -> closing -> closed
142-```
143-
144-Rules:
145-
146-- `connect` is valid only in `idle`. Concurrent or repeated calls fail with
147-  `invalidState`; they never start a second socket.
148-- Host policy is the sole exit from `awaitingHostDecision`. Authentication
149-  cannot begin before an accept decision.
150-- `connect` returns only in `ready`, after authentication succeeds. The returned
151-  session contains opaque session/lane identity, accepted host-key evidence,
152:  negotiated algorithms, and the current key-exchange generation.
153-- New channel opens are accepted only in `ready`. Calls made while `rekeying`
154-  wait in a bounded, cancellable open queue and consume their existing
155-  channel-open deadline. They are never silently opened on another lane.
156-- Existing channels remain logically valid during `rekeying`. Reads/writes MAY
157-  suspend behind engine KEX processing, but all adapter-owned read/write buffers
158-  remain within their configured bounds and payload bytes are neither lost,
159-  duplicated, nor reordered.
160-- A connection-fatal error stops new work, fails all pending operations with the
161-  same connection scope/cause, and enters teardown exactly once.
162-- `close` is idempotent from every state. It rejects new work, cancels timers and
163-  pending opens, asks channels to close, enforces the injected close grace
164-  deadline, force-releases remaining engine resources, closes the socket, and
165-  returns only after ownership counts are zero. Caller cancellation does not
166-  interrupt this cleanup.
167-
168-The linked lifecycle diagram is
169-`TASK-260715-2ny6z4_connection-lifecycle.puml`.
170-
171-## 6. Host verification and authentication
172-
173-### 6.1 Evidence
174-
175-After transport key exchange exposes the server key and before any user-auth
176-request or credential lookup, the adapter MUST call `SSHHostKeyPolicy.evaluate`
177-with:
178-
179-- canonical profile hostname and connected SSH endpoint;
180-- exact negotiated host-key algorithm name;
181-- exact SSH wire-format host-key bytes, copied into immutable `Data`;
182-- canonical OpenSSH-style `SHA256:<base64-without-padding>` fingerprint computed
183-  from those bytes;
184-- lane identity and the profile's opaque trust-record reference.
185-
186-Evidence supports Ed25519, ECDSA, and RSA-SHA2 algorithm names without changing
187-the public shape. Unknown algorithms remain representable and are rejectable.
188-Raw bytes and the fingerprint are available to policy even when the decision is
189-reject.
190-
191-### 6.2 Policy decisions
192-
193-The common decisions are:
194-
195-- `acceptFirstUse(explicitTrustRecord)`: valid only when a prior explicit user
196-  trust action created the supplied record; the packet-tunnel transport never
197-  prompts or silently trusts;
198-- `acceptMatch(trustRecord)`: algorithm and fingerprint match an approved record;
199-- `rejectUnknown`: no approved record and no explicit first-use trust;
200-- `rejectChanged`: the canonical host has approved history but evidence differs;
201-- `rejectAlgorithm`: policy forbids or does not understand the host-key algorithm;
202-- `rejectPolicy`: any other explicit policy denial.
203-
204-`rejectChanged` is a high-severity, non-retryable-without-configuration-change
205-connection failure. It closes the socket before credential access, never opens
206-a channel, never reconnects into acceptance, and cannot be converted to first
207-use by an adapter. All lanes for one higher-level session are checked against
208-the same approved identity by the injected policy.
209-
210-### 6.3 Authentication
211-
212-Only after acceptance may the adapter request credentials and enter
213-`authenticating`. Baseline authentication is noninteractive public-key auth.
214-The common outcome enum distinguishes:
215-
216-- `success`;
217-- `rejectedByServer`;
218-- `methodUnavailable`;
219-- `keyAlgorithmUnavailable`;
220-- `credentialUnavailable`;
221-- `credentialInteractionRequired` (locked/passphrase/UI action required);
222-- `signatureFailed`;
223-- `cancelled` and `timedOut`.
224-
225-Ed25519 and approved fallback fixtures (ECDSA and/or RSA-SHA2 according to the
226-product matrix) are expressed through common algorithm identifiers. Password
227-prompts and agent forwarding are not fallback behavior. No auth rejection may
228-be misreported as a host-key rejection or generic network failure.
229-
230-The linked ordering diagram is
231-`TASK-260715-2ny6z4_host-verification-sequence.puml`.
232-
233-## 7. Channel open and byte-stream semantics
234-
235-### 7.1 Direct TCP
236-
237-`openDirectTCPIP` transmits the caller's destination and originator in the RFC
238-4254 `direct-tcpip` request. Destination host and nonzero port are required.
239-Originator host and port are explicit immutable input and are sent verbatim; the
240-adapter neither substitutes the SSH peer nor derives routing policy from them.
241-Neither endpoint is emitted to default logs, errors, metrics, or event labels.
242-
243-Open success returns a channel in `open`; server rejection preserves the SSH
244-reason category in a candidate-neutral `channelOpenRejected` code. Timeout or
245-cancellation after a remote channel ID exists performs best-effort close and
246-unregisters the partial channel before returning.
247-
248-### 7.2 Exec
249-
250-`openExecChannel` opens an SSH session channel, sends exactly one exec request,
251-and returns only after acceptance. It exposes stdin through `writeSome` and
252-`finishWriting`, stdout through `read`, stderr through `readStandardError`, and
--
348-The adapter MUST use the configured policy for direct and exec channels; the
349-libssh2 direct helper's 2 MiB default and NIOSSH's audited 16 MiB target do not
350-override it.
351-
352-`receiveWindow()` returns a consistent snapshot containing initial value, cap,
353-remaining protocol credit, buffered unread bytes, delivered-but-not-yet-returned
354-credit, adjustment count, and cumulative adjustment bytes. A typed
355-`windowAdjusted(channel, before, amount, after, cap)` event makes each adjust
356-independently observable without payload or endpoint data.
357-
358:## 10. Rekey and keepalive
359-
360:### 10.1 Rekey
361-
362-`SSHRekeyPolicy` supplies a positive protected-byte threshold per direction, a
363-positive elapsed-time threshold, and a rekey timeout. Protected bytes are
364-counted after SSH packet encoding and before socket write, or after socket read
365-and before SSH packet decoding; TCP/IP overhead is excluded. Either direction
366-reaching the byte threshold triggers client rekey. Time uses the injected
367-monotonic clock from the last successful key exchange, independent of traffic.
368-
369-Client triggers are `byteThreshold`, `timeThreshold`, and explicit `test` or
370-`manual`. Server `SSH_MSG_KEXINIT` is `serverInitiated`. At most one KEX runs per
371-connection. Concurrent triggers coalesce into that KEX while retaining a set of
372-observed reasons. A successful KEX increments `keyExchangeGeneration`, resets
373-protected-byte counters and the monotonic origin, and emits started/succeeded
374-events. The explicit test trigger uses the same production path; no test-only
375-engine symbol crosses the adapter boundary.
376-
377-Cancellation of a caller waiting on `requestRekey` does not attempt to roll back
378-an already-started connection-global KEX; the call returns `cancelled` while the
379-transport continues and reports the eventual result. Rekey timeout, protocol
380-failure, or key validation failure is connection-fatal because crypto state is
381-uncertain. Existing channels then fail with that lane-local connection error;
382-cross-lane recovery belongs to the lane pool.
383-
384-During KEX, no payload is lost, duplicated, or reordered; read/write suspension
385-is bounded as in section 8, and new opens wait as in section 5. The linked
386-channel diagram is `TASK-260715-2ny6z4_channel-window-rekey-sequence.puml`.
387-
388:### 10.2 Keepalive
389-
390:`SSHKeepalivePolicy` supplies interval, reply timeout, and allowed consecutive
391:misses. Automatic keepalive uses an SSH global request that requires a reply.
392-Its schedule is based on monotonic time and is not reset merely by application
393-payload; SSH liveness remains distinct from application traffic. A manual
394:`sendKeepalive` exercises the same path and returns measured monotonic RTT.
395-
396:Only one keepalive is outstanding. A due keepalive during KEX is deferred until
397-KEX succeeds without creating an unbounded queue. Success, timeout, and RTT are
398:observable. Exhausting the miss allowance is connection-fatal. Keepalive never
399-claims to detect physical path changes; that belongs to higher lifecycle code.
400-
401-## 11. Timeouts and cancellation points
402-
403-`SSHTimeoutPolicy` supplies separate values for DNS/endpoint resolution, TCP
404-connect, initial KEX, host decision, credential lookup, authentication, channel
405:open, write-credit wait, explicit rekey, keepalive reply, exec exit, upload, and
406-graceful channel/transport close. Long-lived reads have no implicit idle timeout;
407-a caller may supply a deadline/cancel its task. Final durations are out of scope.
408-
409-Every async operation checks cancellation before starting and immediately after
410-each suspension/readiness wake. Required cancellable waits are: resolution, TCP
411-connect/readiness, KEX, host policy, credential lookup, authentication, channel
412-open, read, write credit, EOF flush, exec exit, upload-source read, rekey wait,
413:keepalive reply, and graceful close wait.
414-
415-| Cancellation/timeout site | Required scope and cleanup |
416-|---|---|
417-| Resolve/TCP/KEX/host/auth during `connect` | Connection-fatal; close partial socket/session; no credential access after host rejection |

